Hi Jamie, if you have only a single sex in your data then the sex-specific likelihood is not going to work.  You cannot estimate parameters specific to females if there are no female observations.  Now, if you use multi-session models/data and some of your sessions do not have females, that should be okay if your parameters are not session specific.

A better approach to testing your data would be to work with a session that has both sexes, or to remove sex as an individual covariate when fitting your model.  We probably need an error message for when only a single sex is observed and oSCR tries to fit the sex-specific likelihood.

